#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481743} :activity/id #uuid "4e0c95fe-b957-5da5-82b5-741cf5414583" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050615 :topic/id #uuid "58ea9fa1-e1b7-56f7-a58d-173ed320b2af" :topic/math-academy-id 6369} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481696 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07208325-b6fe-56ff-ab78-18466949f3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445913} {:answer/id #uuid "7dcba25b-c390-5edc-b740-7305051f595a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $7$ vertically." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445915} {:answer/id #uuid "9b26e88d-f446-59c8-b01e-ca9d9e592906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $7$ horizontally." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445917} {:answer/id #uuid "c94dc5ec-cf48-51ff-9ce3-01c9fcc65d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the left." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445919} {:answer/id #uuid "ff77f603-3667-5d57-9f2f-4b85ac75f514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the right." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445922}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445919} :answer-field/id #uuid "0524c331-6564-52ef-aba2-9b3e8fe558a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5d66a4d-16c1-5cfd-be68-7c518337e0a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-332062" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the left."} :step/id #uuid "4de91bb7-3162-59ca-b8a7-380041a9f451" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481742}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481699 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5812230e-36ca-591f-b89d-48a8b7e1dcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{7}{(s-5)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445914} {:answer/id #uuid "83050161-c9bb-5cd0-b5d2-7a04f0e38764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445916} {:answer/id #uuid "a6286f17-855d-5d36-bc99-ac311ecdbde2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445918} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1cbeb1-e8a7-518d-a6fb-cbc0586e7fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s+5)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445920} {:answer/id #uuid "f81a2466-a55c-5f7e-b7c2-eccdac0d7ed6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{14}{(s+5)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445921}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445918} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbbbb7ac-d5cd-51d2-b97c-84f0232078de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "34e7ccd4-a685-5844-a89f-5c57b735778f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332187" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}$ for $s>5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{2{e}^{-3t}+7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{2{e}^{-3t}\\}+L\\{7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =2L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\}+7L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} & =\\frac{2}{(s-5)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =2L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\}+7L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3}+7\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-5)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>5.$"} :step/id #uuid "58a277dc-7f40-5be3-81ea-a2342d28f19a"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481735 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186304946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16979a0a-5fab-5b5c-86c2-4f69494ac5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}d(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302602} {:answer/id #uuid "2a895488-234e-5a29-b81f-90f3846bbf81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{st}\\cdot {e}^{9t}d(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302726} {:answer/id #uuid "8b629cb7-7b7c-531d-8fad-665f318f2f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045